Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.97,0:00:03.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This needle has pointed\Nnorth your whole life.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:04.37,0:00:06.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But the seafloor remembers further back.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:07.26,0:00:09.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}North and south have swapped.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.59,0:00:12.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Again and again, with no schedule.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.54,0:00:17.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That needle follows a {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nthat wraps the whole planet.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.37,0:00:18.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It feels permanent.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:19.22,0:00:20.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It is not.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.07,0:00:24.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At ocean ridges, fresh\Nlava cools into rock.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.91,0:00:28.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Tiny magnetic grains lock\Nin the {\c&H3468EB&}field's{\c&HF7F4F2&} direction.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.75,0:00:32.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The seafloor spreads,\Nand each flip starts a new stripe.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.31,0:00:38.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Five million years, about twenty\Nflips, and wildly uneven gaps.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:39.87,0:00:41.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So, two questions.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:41.94,0:00:43.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}When will it flip next?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:43.67,0:00:45.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&} does it flip?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.97,0:00:47.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which one can we answer?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:49.83,0:00:54.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In a simple computer model of a\Nflipping {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&}, the first is hopeless.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:54.56,0:00:56.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The second is not.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:56.92,0:01:00.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Finding out why takes us\Nthrough liquid metal and chaos.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:00.73,0:01:04.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then to {\c&HF09CB4&}machines{\c&HF7F4F2&} that dream up\Nsnapshots of magnetic fields.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:05.20,0:01:09.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}They must obey some laws exactly,\Nand others only on average.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:11.17,0:01:13.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How often does the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} flip?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:13.42,0:01:18.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A learner's map of the MilaDy project,\Nfrom a compass to today's research.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:21.24,0:01:23.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Should the first\Nsimulations target {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&}
Dialogue: 0,0:01:23.86,0:01:27.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the field flips, or the warning\Nsigns just before a flip?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.57,0:01:35.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Is there a giant magnet\Nhidden inside the Earth?
Dialogue: 0,0:01:36.10,0:01:40.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Deep down, the outer core\Nis an ocean of liquid iron.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.30,0:01:45.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Left alone, a {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} there would fade\Naway within tens of thousands of years.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:45.70,0:01:48.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet Earth's field is\Nseveral billion years old.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:49.24,0:01:52.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the moving iron must keep\Nmaking it, all the time.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:54.00,0:01:58.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Take a bar magnet: north at\None end, south at the other.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:58.39,0:01:59.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Cut it in half.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:59.67,0:02:03.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}You get two magnets, each\Nwith a north and a south.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:03.83,0:02:07.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No one has ever found\Na lone magnetic pole.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:07.78,0:02:11.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&H3468EB&}Field lines{\c&HF7F4F2&} say the same:\Nthey never start or stop.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:12.07,0:02:14.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Draw any box, and count.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:14.39,0:02:16.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}As many lines go in as come out.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:17.18,0:02:18.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Keep this box in mind.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.97,0:02:25.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A bicycle dynamo turns motion into\Nelectricity, using a built-in magnet.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:25.42,0:02:29.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The Earth has to make its\Nown. The trick: a loop.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:30.21,0:02:33.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Hold a microphone near its own\Nspeaker, and turn up the {\c&H7AAF1B&}gain{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:34.32,0:02:37.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below a certain point, a whisper fades.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:37.64,0:02:40.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Past it, the loop feeds itself.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.15,0:02:46.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now spin a copper disc wired\Nto a coil around its axle.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:47.00,0:02:49.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Current makes {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nand field makes current.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:50.26,0:02:54.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Spin it faster and faster.\NDoes the field grow bit by bit,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:54.37,0:02:56.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}or not at all until some speed?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:00.63,0:03:03.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not at all, until a threshold.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:03.47,0:03:06.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Past it, each turn multiplies a tiny
Dialogue: 0,0:03:06.31,0:03:10.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&H3468EB&}seed field{\c&HF7F4F2&} by the same factor:\Nexponential growth.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:11.11,0:03:14.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Just past the threshold, growth is slow.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:14.45,0:03:18.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then the field brakes the\Ndisc, and growth stops.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:19.50,0:03:21.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Who pays for all this?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:21.46,0:03:25.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A motor drives the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&};\Nin the Earth, convection does.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:26.31,0:03:30.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The flow stretches the field;\Nthe field brakes the flow.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:30.81,0:03:36.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Once growth stops, every watt that\Ngoes in leaves as heat, on average.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:36.63,0:03:41.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A dynamo turns motion into\Nfield; it creates no energy.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:42.37,0:03:46.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Earth's {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} points one way today.\NWhich way should it prefer?
Dialogue: 0,0:03:50.08,0:03:54.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Reverse every field line:\Nthe dynamo's equations read the same.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:54.79,0:04:00.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So, neither. The two directions\Nare mirror twins, equally lawful.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:00.46,0:04:06.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which one you get is history, not law:\Nin the disc, the seed's sign decides.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:06.74,0:04:09.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And nothing in the laws forbids a flip.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:10.77,0:04:15.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Moving iron, a loop with a threshold,\Nno favourite direction.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:15.85,0:04:19.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But the disc had wires;\Nliquid iron has none.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:19.95,0:04:23.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then what motion can do it, and how fast?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:24.37,0:04:27.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near the threshold,\Nshould dynamo simulations
Dialogue: 0,0:04:27.40,0:04:30.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}start from a tiny seed,\Na strong one, or both?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:36.27,0:04:39.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}What kind of motion can\Ngrow a {\c&H3468EB&}magnetic field{\c&HF7F4F2&}?
Dialogue: 0,0:04:40.02,0:04:45.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In a perfect conductor, field lines\Nwould be threads frozen into the fluid.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:45.27,0:04:49.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Wherever the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} stretches a thread,\Nthe field along it grows stronger.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:50.58,0:04:54.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Real metals let the threads\Nsmudge, like spreading ink.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:54.44,0:04:58.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The smudging speed is set by the\Nmetal's {\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic diffusivity{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:58.37,0:05:00.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Same flow, two metals.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:00.82,0:05:04.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A good conductor smudges slowly:\Nthe threads stay sharp.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:05.04,0:05:09.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A poor one smudges fast:\Nthe threads blur away.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:10.07,0:05:15.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One number keeps the score:\Nthe {\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Reynolds number{\c&HF7F4F2&}, Rm.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:15.44,0:05:21.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It is the {\c&HE58739&}flow's speed{\c&HF7F4F2&}, times its size,\Ndivided by the magnetic diffusivity.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:21.57,0:05:25.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Large Rm: stretching outpaces smudging.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:25.69,0:05:28.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Small Rm: smudging wins.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:29.38,0:05:33.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Turn the Rm knob up,\Nand watch a weak seed field.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:33.87,0:05:36.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At low Rm, the seed dies away.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:37.19,0:05:43.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Past a threshold, the growth rate crosses\Nzero and the seed grows, like the squeal.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:44.50,0:05:47.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Laboratory dynamos use liquid sodium.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:47.44,0:05:49.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It flows as easily as water.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:49.96,0:05:54.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But a field smudges in it about a hundred\Nthousand times faster than its swirls do.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:55.20,0:06:01.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Their ratio, one in a hundred thousand,\Nis the {\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Prandtl number{\c&HF7F4F2&}, Pm.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:02.06,0:06:05.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Reaching an Rm of a few\Ntens takes violent stirring.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:06.33,0:06:11.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The flow's {\c&H7AAF1B&}Reynolds number{\c&HF7F4F2&}, the gauge\Nof turbulence, reaches millions.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:11.21,0:06:14.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So every laboratory dynamo is turbulent.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:15.34,0:06:17.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Only three liquid-sodium experiments have
Dialogue: 0,0:06:17.70,0:06:20.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}made their own field,\Neach a different kind.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:21.04,0:06:26.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In Riga, a corkscrew flow made a field\Nthat oscillates and slowly turns.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:26.54,0:06:32.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In Karlsruhe, fifty-two spiralling cells\Nmade a steady field, close to a {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.95,0:06:37.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And only VKS, at Cadarache,\Nmade a dipole that can flip.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:38.47,0:06:42.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A fourth, DRESDYN in Dresden,\Naims to join them:
Dialogue: 0,0:06:42.15,0:06:45.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a spinning cylinder\Nwhose axis slowly turns.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:46.90,0:06:52.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Inside VKS: a cylinder holding a hundred\Nand fifty litres of liquid sodium.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:52.83,0:06:58.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At its ends, two bladed discs, the\Nimpellers, spin in opposite directions.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:59.43,0:07:02.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Should it matter what metal\Nthe discs are made of?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:05.35,0:07:10.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}With stainless steel discs, there\Nwas no dynamo, even at full speed.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:10.44,0:07:15.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Swap in discs of soft iron,\Nand a dipole grows along the axis.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:17.21,0:07:21.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the threshold depends on more\Nthan Rm: materials count too.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:22.84,0:07:25.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now spin the two discs\Nat different speeds.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:26.22,0:07:30.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At some settings, the dipole flips\Nagain and again, with no schedule.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:31.28,0:07:34.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Like the compass needle at the\Nstart, but every minute or two.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:35.69,0:07:38.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each flip itself takes\Nonly about five seconds.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:39.26,0:07:45.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Uneven stripes, like the seafloor's, but\Nwritten in minutes, not millions of years.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:46.40,0:07:49.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Simulations cannot reach sodium's tiny {\c&H7AAF1B&}Pm{\c&HF7F4F2&}:
Dialogue: 0,0:07:49.29,0:07:53.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}which Pm values should the first\Nthree-dimensional runs cover?
Dialogue: 0,0:07:59.34,0:08:04.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Riga, Karlsruhe and VKS crowned\Na hunt of nearly a century.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:04.71,0:08:05.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is its map.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:06.66,0:08:12.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lane one, the Earth's record: lavas\Nmagnetized backwards, nineteen oh six.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:13.26,0:08:16.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Seafloor stripes, nineteen sixty-three.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:16.78,0:08:21.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then flips as chance events,\Nwhose odds change over the ages.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:22.50,0:08:24.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lane two, theory.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:24.83,0:08:30.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen nineteen: a moving, conducting\Nfluid might keep the Sun's field alive.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:30.93,0:08:36.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen thirty-three: no flow can sustain\Na field that is symmetric around an axis.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:37.65,0:08:42.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen fifty-eight: the first\Nproven examples of flows that can.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:42.58,0:08:45.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In the seventies came simple dynamo flows,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:45.40,0:08:48.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}later the blueprints\Nof Riga and Karlsruhe.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:49.67,0:08:52.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lane three: building a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:52.78,0:08:57.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen sixty-three: a model\Ndynamo of spinning iron cylinders.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:57.96,0:09:03.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen ninety-nine: sodium makes its\Nown field, in Riga, then Karlsruhe.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:04.91,0:09:08.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two thousand six: VKS,\Nonly with iron discs,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:08.68,0:09:13.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}then the first flips of a\Nturbulent sodium dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:14.74,0:09:16.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lane four, computers.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:17.34,0:09:21.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen eighty-one: simulated\Nturbulence grows dynamos.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:22.13,0:09:27.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen ninety-five: a simulated\NEarth's core reverses its field.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:27.48,0:09:31.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Ringed pins mark papers co-signed\Nby this project's supervisors.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:32.57,0:09:38.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen ninety-seven: two simulated\Ncounter-rotating eddies make a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:38.38,0:09:43.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two thousand five: such dynamos\Nstill work at a {\c&H7AAF1B&}Pm{\c&HF7F4F2&} of one hundredth.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:44.37,0:09:50.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}From twenty sixteen, two codes simulate\Nthe VKS tank, iron impellers included.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:51.17,0:09:53.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Twenty twenty-six: both make it flip,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:53.69,0:09:57.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}in the runs explored only\Nwith conducting surroundings.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:58.03,0:10:03.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Their reference run: a {\c&H7AAF1B&}Reynolds number{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nnear sixteen hundred, not millions.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:04.50,0:10:07.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lane five: a few dials instead of a tank.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:08.39,0:10:12.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nineteen fifty-eight: two coupled\Ndisc dynamos already flip.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:13.38,0:10:19.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Twenty twelve: three dials that flip by\Nchaos alone; we will play with them soon.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:20.41,0:10:25.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Twenty twenty-six: four dials\Nfitted to the simulated VKS flips,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:25.14,0:10:28.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}by a method that finds equations in data.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:29.54,0:10:31.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}MilaDy began in twenty twenty-six,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:31.65,0:10:35.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}bringing machine learning where\Ntanks, computers and dials meet.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:36.54,0:10:40.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet the data-driven lane is\Nthin: in twenty twenty-two,
Dialogue: 0,0:10:40.41,0:10:46.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the learning methods one study tried could\Nnot reliably foresee a geomagnetic flip.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:47.33,0:10:50.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The internship and a\Nproposed thesis add a bet:
Dialogue: 0,0:10:50.52,0:10:54.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&HF09CB4&}machines{\c&HF7F4F2&} that dream up dynamo\Nfields and obey their physics.
Dialogue: 0,0:10:55.68,0:11:00.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Behind every lane, the same race:\Nstretching against smudging.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:04.74,0:11:09.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the race: the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} stretches\Nthe field, and diffusion smudges it.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:10.15,0:11:13.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Physicists write the very same\Nrace as a single equation.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:14.73,0:11:19.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the left: how fast the\N{\c&H3468EB&}magnetic field{\c&HF7F4F2&} changes, at each point.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:20.10,0:11:22.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First term on the right:\Nthe flow carries the
Dialogue: 0,0:11:22.83,0:11:26.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}field along and stretches\Nit, like the threads.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:26.54,0:11:32.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Second term: diffusion smudges it,\Nat a pace set by the magnetic diffusivity.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:32.81,0:11:35.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A poor conductor smudges fast.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:35.84,0:11:40.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Stretching against smudging:\Nthe whole race, in one line.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:41.24,0:11:45.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Picture the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} as a conveyor\Nbelt that does not feel its cargo.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:45.65,0:11:51.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A weak {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} rides on the imposed flow,\Nand fades or grows exponentially.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:51.71,0:11:56.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On a log scale: a straight line,\Nwhose slope is the growth rate.
Dialogue: 0,0:11:57.03,0:11:59.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But a strong field\Npushes back on the flow,
Dialogue: 0,0:11:59.86,0:12:04.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}as it braked the copper disc:\Nthe line bends into a plateau.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:04.48,0:12:07.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}These are the kinematic\Nand dynamic regimes.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:07.70,0:12:09.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Flips live on the plateau.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:10.94,0:12:14.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To test ideas, physicists\Nneed a {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} simple enough
Dialogue: 0,0:12:14.40,0:12:18.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}to write on one line,\Nyet able to make a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:18.40,0:12:21.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One classic choice: the ABC flow.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:22.53,0:12:25.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It fills space with\Nidentical twisting rooms.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:25.90,0:12:28.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three crossed winds make\Nthe fluid corkscrew:
Dialogue: 0,0:12:28.84,0:12:31.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}it spins around the direction it moves.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:32.08,0:12:34.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This box returns higher on our climb.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:35.84,0:12:41.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now turn one {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob{\c&HF7F4F2&} on this flow: Rm.\NTurning it up means less diffusion.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:42.46,0:12:45.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Less smudging should\Nonly help the stretching.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:45.85,0:12:49.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So once the dynamo switches\Non, it stays on. Right?
Dialogue: 0,0:12:53.63,0:12:56.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is the growth rate,\Ncomputed at twenty-four settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:12:57.24,0:13:03.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below about nine, every {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} fades. Then\Ngrowth turns positive: the dynamo is on.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:04.47,0:13:07.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near seventeen and a half,\Nit switches off again.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:07.79,0:13:10.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Above about twenty-seven, it comes back.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:10.53,0:13:15.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not one threshold, but windows: turning\Na knob further does not always help.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:16.20,0:13:19.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It agrees with published\Nresults. No machine
Dialogue: 0,0:13:19.28,0:13:22.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}learning yet: this only tests the solver.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:23.74,0:13:27.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Real dynamos have not\None {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob{\c&HF7F4F2&}, but a panel.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:27.48,0:13:30.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How hard the metal is stirred;\Nhow well it conducts.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:31.11,0:13:34.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}What the discs are made\Nof: remember the iron.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:34.37,0:13:39.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How unequal their speeds are:\Nthat is when VKS flipped.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:39.39,0:13:44.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For DRESDYN, a precessing tank:\Nhow fast its axis turns.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:45.24,0:13:49.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Even the wall counts: in VKS\Nsimulations, changing only its
Dialogue: 0,0:13:49.29,0:13:53.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}magnetic condition raised the\Nthreshold by about forty percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:55.00,0:13:59.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Every turbulent dynamo simulation\Nin three dimensions is very costly.
Dialogue: 0,0:13:59.85,0:14:02.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So only a handful of knob\Nsettings get simulated.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:03.40,0:14:09.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each one is a mountain of data: a single\Nsnapshot can weigh more than a gigabyte.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:10.06,0:14:12.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each run holds only a few flips.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:12.70,0:14:15.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And between the dots,\Nthere is nothing at all.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:16.67,0:14:19.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Those gaps are where this film lives.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:19.75,0:14:22.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The study behind this\Nfilm asks three things.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:23.45,0:14:26.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One: can a {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} learn\Nthe whole family of fields,
Dialogue: 0,0:14:27.00,0:14:30.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}even at knob settings between the dots?
Dialogue: 0,0:14:30.72,0:14:34.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two: can it respect\Neach law where it holds,
Dialogue: 0,0:14:34.12,0:14:38.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}exactly in every field,\Nor {\c&H00A1ED&}on average{\c&HF7F4F2&} over many fields?
Dialogue: 0,0:14:39.32,0:14:43.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three: can it tell us\N{\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&} the field flips?
Dialogue: 0,0:14:43.87,0:14:47.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To find out, the study's\Nplan climbs a staircase,
Dialogue: 0,0:14:47.09,0:14:50.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}from three numbers to the\Nshapes of real experiments.
Dialogue: 0,0:14:51.65,0:14:55.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One honest note: this\Nindependent study is prospective,
Dialogue: 0,0:14:55.44,0:14:58.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}built on small experiments\Non an ordinary computer,
Dialogue: 0,0:14:58.96,0:15:03.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and not endorsed by MilaDy,\Nits partners or the supervisors.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:05.07,0:15:10.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}An equation, windows, a panel\Nof knobs, a handful of dots.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:10.63,0:15:12.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Full simulations cost too much,
Dialogue: 0,0:15:12.59,0:15:16.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so we start with a three-dial\Ncaricature that already flips.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:17.04,0:15:19.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Can anyone say when it flips next?
Dialogue: 0,0:15:20.60,0:15:23.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}With only a handful of runs,\Nshould the {\c&H7AAF1B&}settings{\c&HF7F4F2&}
Dialogue: 0,0:15:23.47,0:15:26.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}be spread evenly,\Nor packed near each threshold?
Dialogue: 0,0:15:31.90,0:15:36.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is the caricature:\Nthree dials, linked by springs.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:36.50,0:15:39.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One is the {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nthe north-south part of the field.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:40.27,0:15:43.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One is the {\c&H3468EB&}quadrupole{\c&HF7F4F2&}, a four-pole part.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:43.87,0:15:47.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The third is a {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} pattern,\Ndriven by a knob, Gamma.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:48.67,0:15:52.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three short equations, from\NGissinger, tie them together.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:53.14,0:15:56.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Flip both field dials,\Nand the equations read the same.
Dialogue: 0,0:15:57.23,0:16:01.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three dials, three numbers:\Nthe bottom step of the staircase.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:02.87,0:16:07.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Use two dials as a map:\N{\c&H3468EB&}quadrupole{\c&HF7F4F2&} across, dipole up.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:08.57,0:16:13.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}With the knob low, the state\Ncircles in one whirlpool, and stays.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:13.69,0:16:17.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A mirror twin circles the\Nmirror whirlpool: dipole down.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:18.31,0:16:22.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each start is trapped.\NThe dipole never changes sign.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:23.44,0:16:24.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now turn {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} up.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:25.22,0:16:31.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Just above zero point eight six, the\Nwhirlpools touch: a narrow corridor opens.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:31.94,0:16:37.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The state lingers on one side,\Nthen slips through. The {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} flips.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:37.82,0:16:42.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nothing here is random. So,\Nwill the flips come like clockwork?
Dialogue: 0,0:16:45.84,0:16:49.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. Long waits and\Nshort ones, irregularly.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:49.77,0:16:53.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The dipole's sign prints uneven\Nstripes, like the seafloor.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:54.70,0:16:59.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now count: one tick each\Ntime the {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} crosses zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:16:59.75,0:17:04.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not every tick is a flip: an excursion\Ncan cross and come straight back.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:05.01,0:17:08.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Ticks per unit of time: the {\c&H00A1ED&}crossing rate{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:08.91,0:17:12.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Up to zero point eight six: silence.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:12.52,0:17:16.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Just above, about two ticks\Nper thousand time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:16.65,0:17:19.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} equal to one, about eleven.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:20.67,0:17:26.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Set {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} at zero point nine, and\Nlaunch two copies, one millionth apart.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:26.53,0:17:31.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}We know the state to six decimals.\NCan we predict the date of the next flip?
Dialogue: 0,0:17:35.34,0:17:38.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At first, the copies move as one.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:38.22,0:17:41.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But their gap nearly triples\Nevery eleven time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:42.17,0:17:45.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}After about a hundred and fifty,\Nthey disagree completely.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:46.61,0:17:50.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The average wait between crossings\Nis longer: over two hundred.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:51.31,0:17:55.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each extra decimal buys only\Nabout twenty-five more time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:17:55.55,0:18:01.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the forecast fades before the average\Nwait ends. Only the statistics survive.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:02.37,0:18:05.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The path is lost,\Nso leave the shutter open.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:06.00,0:18:09.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each pass leaves a little\Nlight: a glow builds.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:10.12,0:18:14.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A copy started on the other side\Nfirst paints the mirror image.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:14.51,0:18:17.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Given time, both paint the same glow.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:18.58,0:18:23.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where does the {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} spend its\Ntime? Two humps, the same for both.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:23.99,0:18:27.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This reproducible glow is\Nthe stationary distribution.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:27.98,0:18:32.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below the threshold, each\Nstart paints its own whirlpool.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:33.07,0:18:37.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now film the trajectory,\None frame every two time units.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:37.84,0:18:39.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Neighbouring frames look alike.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:40.35,0:18:42.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Cut the film into frames.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:42.36,0:18:46.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Drop them in a shoebox, shake,\Nand throw away the timestamps.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:47.39,0:18:51.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pour the box out:\Nthe frames redraw the same glow.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:52.49,0:18:56.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each frame is a snapshot:\Nthree dial readings, nothing more.
Dialogue: 0,0:18:57.05,0:19:00.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It carries no timestamp and no time order.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:00.36,0:19:04.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet the shoebox, plus the equations,\Nanswers many questions.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:04.77,0:19:09.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Averages, energy budgets, even\Nhow often the dipole crosses zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:10.80,0:19:14.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pick one photo. Its three\Ndial readings give its energy.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:15.03,0:19:19.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}From that photo alone, the equations\Ntell how fast its energy changes.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:20.25,0:19:23.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Drop one marble per photo at that value.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:24.00,0:19:29.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Some photos are gaining energy,\Nothers losing it. The pile spreads wide.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:29.29,0:19:31.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet its average sits at zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:31.82,0:19:35.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Energy in equals energy out, on average.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:35.85,0:19:38.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Like a bank account: over the years,
Dialogue: 0,0:19:38.41,0:19:42.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}income matches spending,\Nyet each day gains or loses.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:42.86,0:19:48.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This is a {\c&H00A1ED&}law of the crowd{\c&HF7F4F2&}. It holds\Nfor the shoebox, not for each photo.
Dialogue: 0,0:19:49.40,0:19:52.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How many independent snapshots\Ndoes each setting need,
Dialogue: 0,0:19:52.64,0:19:55.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and how far apart must they be saved?
Dialogue: 0,0:20:00.44,0:20:04.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A puzzle. How many cars\Ncross this line each hour?
Dialogue: 0,0:20:04.82,0:20:10.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No clock. Just one photo from above,\Nwhere every speedometer can be read.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:10.95,0:20:14.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Can one photo, with no time\Nin it, give you a rate?
Dialogue: 0,0:20:17.90,0:20:21.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It can. Draw a thin strip around the line.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:22.45,0:20:27.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A fast car spends little time inside,\Nso catching one there counts for more.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:28.41,0:20:32.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Add up the speeds of the cars inside,\Nand divide by the strip's width.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:33.27,0:20:37.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On average, that is how\Nmany cars cross per hour.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:38.07,0:20:42.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the shoebox. Spread its\Nphotos out by their {\c&H3468EB&}dipole{\c&HF7F4F2&} reading.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:42.60,0:20:46.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The line to cross is zero:\Ndraw a thin strip around it.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:47.59,0:20:50.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each photo also needs a speedometer.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:50.49,0:20:53.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No clock required:\Nthe dipole's equation turns
Dialogue: 0,0:20:53.39,0:20:56.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the photo's three dial\Nreadings into a speed.
Dialogue: 0,0:20:56.97,0:21:01.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Up or down, both count,\Nso keep only its size.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:02.08,0:21:03.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Add the speeds inside the strip,
Dialogue: 0,0:21:03.95,0:21:07.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}then divide by its width and\Nby the total number of photos.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:08.05,0:21:09.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Out comes the {\c&H00A1ED&}crossing rate{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:10.33,0:21:13.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Shrink the strip to nothing,\Nand for well-behaved
Dialogue: 0,0:21:13.67,0:21:16.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}systems the recipe is\Nexact: Rice's formula.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:17.44,0:21:21.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The catch: few photos land\Nin a thin strip. Here,
Dialogue: 0,0:21:21.30,0:21:24.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}about two dozen out of eight thousand.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:25.67,0:21:27.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now test it against the truth.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:27.94,0:21:29.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At forty-one settings of {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:21:29.83,0:21:33.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}long simulations count the\Ncrossings as they happen.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:33.60,0:21:38.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Rice gets only the true snapshots,\Nshuffled, plus the equations.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:39.54,0:21:41.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The two curves almost coincide.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:42.37,0:21:45.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Shade the gap,\Nand divide by the area under
Dialogue: 0,0:21:45.08,0:21:47.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the counted curve: the {\c&H00A1ED&}cumulative error{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:48.32,0:21:51.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Four percent near the threshold,\Nsix percent above it.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:52.27,0:21:54.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A good sign, on three chaotic dials that
Dialogue: 0,0:21:54.80,0:21:57.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}are not even guaranteed\Nto be well-behaved.
Dialogue: 0,0:21:59.30,0:22:03.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One caution: Rice counts\Ncrossings, not reversals.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:03.98,0:22:09.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}An excursion across zero and back\Nadds two crossings, but no reversal.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:09.49,0:22:12.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To isolate reversals, the PhD plan counts
Dialogue: 0,0:22:12.28,0:22:15.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}crossings of a far level,\Non the other side.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:16.84,0:22:19.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So Rice needs only\Nsnapshots and the equations,
Dialogue: 0,0:22:20.06,0:22:23.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}but plenty of snapshots, and cheap ones.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:23.58,0:22:28.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet real dynamo simulations exist\Nat only a few {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob settings{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:28.49,0:22:31.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Between the dots,\Nthere is no shoebox at all.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:32.33,0:22:35.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How do you get snapshots at\Na setting nobody simulated?
Dialogue: 0,0:22:37.37,0:22:40.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For VKS, which dipole should Rice count,
Dialogue: 0,0:22:40.11,0:22:44.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and what threshold turns a\Ncrossing into a reversal?
Dialogue: 0,0:22:49.24,0:22:54.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}New snapshots start as pure noise:\Na round cloud of random points.
Dialogue: 0,0:22:55.00,0:23:00.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Over it, the crowd we want:\None point per snapshot. Here,
Dialogue: 0,0:23:00.16,0:23:04.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}two mirror blobs, like the two whirlpools.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:05.03,0:23:07.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now let a {\c&HF09CB4&}wind{\c&HF7F4F2&} blow through the cloud.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:07.83,0:23:12.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each point drifts, bends,\Nand lands inside the shape of the data.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:13.15,0:23:17.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Fresh noise in, fresh snapshots\Nout, as many as you like.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:18.66,0:23:24.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That wind is a {\c&HF09CB4&}generative model{\c&HF7F4F2&}:\Nnew, plausible examples, on demand.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:24.53,0:23:26.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The whole game is learning the wind.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:28.00,0:23:29.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One warning before the formulas.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:30.32,0:23:34.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The wind runs on its own\N{\c&HF09CB4&}transport clock{\c&HF7F4F2&}, from zero to one.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:34.98,0:23:40.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not physical time: at the end,\Nyou hold one snapshot, not a movie.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:41.38,0:23:47.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the {\c&HF09CB4&}wind's arrows{\c&HF7F4F2&} move snapshots, not\Nfluid: they are not the fluid's velocity.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:48.20,0:23:51.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How is the wind learned?\NWith a simple trick.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:51.90,0:23:56.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Keep one coordinate of each point, and\Nlay the transport clock out to the right.
Dialogue: 0,0:23:57.30,0:24:00.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pair each noise point\Nwith a random snapshot;
Dialogue: 0,0:24:00.26,0:24:02.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}join them with a straight string.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:03.01,0:24:06.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Thousands of strings, crossing everywhere.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:06.79,0:24:09.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Pick one place, at one moment.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:10.05,0:24:13.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Several strings pass through,\Npointing different ways.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:13.96,0:24:18.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The network learns one arrow\Nthere: their {\c&HF09CB4&}average direction{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:18.65,0:24:21.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That is the whole training:\Nplain regression,
Dialogue: 0,0:24:21.60,0:24:24.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}like fitting a curve through points.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:25.03,0:24:28.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Follow the averaged wind, not the strings.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:28.42,0:24:32.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The paths bend, and drawn against\Nthe clock, they never cross.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:33.15,0:24:38.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Learn the average exactly, and the\Nnoise lands exactly on the data's crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:39.30,0:24:41.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now give the wind a {\c&H7AAF1B&}knob{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:41.29,0:24:45.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Turn it: the target changes\Nshape, and the {\c&HF09CB4&}cloud{\c&HF7F4F2&} follows.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:45.58,0:24:47.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One network, every setting.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:48.68,0:24:52.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the three dials, a real network\Ntrained at the filled dots.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:53.20,0:24:56.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It was tested at the open dots,\Nnever seen in training.
Dialogue: 0,0:24:57.25,0:25:02.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In between, no theorem promises\Nsuccess: only tests can tell.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:03.00,0:25:05.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Beyond the range, nothing was tested.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:06.53,0:25:12.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At an open dot, the network's {\c&HF09CB4&}snapshots{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Njoin the shoebox, beside simulated ones.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:13.24,0:25:18.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A generated snapshot has no\Ntrue twin, so we compare crowds.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:18.55,0:25:21.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Picture each crowd as a pile of sand.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:21.47,0:25:25.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&} scores\Nhow different the two piles are.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:25.68,0:25:30.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The earth-mover kind counts how\Nmuch sand must move, and how far.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:31.12,0:25:35.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A perfect model, scored against\Nthe data: will it get zero?
Dialogue: 0,0:25:38.72,0:25:43.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. Roll a fair die a thousand\Ntimes, then a thousand more.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:44.07,0:25:48.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Same die, same truth,\Nyet the two histograms differ.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:49.01,0:25:53.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That gap is the {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nabout what a perfect model scores.
Dialogue: 0,0:25:54.10,0:25:59.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Judge every model against it,\Nat equal sample size, never against zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:00.48,0:26:03.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One catch: the samples\Nmust be independent.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:04.30,0:26:08.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Overlap them, and the floor drops\Ntoo low, as the report warns.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:09.94,0:26:12.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which statistics must the\Ngenerated fields match,
Dialogue: 0,0:26:12.86,0:26:16.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and how close to the noise\Nfloor counts as success?
Dialogue: 0,0:26:21.27,0:26:24.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Physics hands the machine\Ntwo kinds of laws.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:24.42,0:26:26.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Some hold for every single snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:27.31,0:26:29.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Others hold only for the crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:29.88,0:26:32.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two shelves, still empty.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:32.84,0:26:37.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the three dials, at a\N{\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} never seen in training.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:37.42,0:26:39.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Grey: true snapshots.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:40.03,0:26:42.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Violet: a plain {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:42.84,0:26:44.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The shape is right.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:44.97,0:26:46.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now weigh the sides.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:47.06,0:26:50.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The truth is balanced; the machine is not.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:50.57,0:26:53.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Only about four in ten of\Nits snapshots point up.
Dialogue: 0,0:26:54.14,0:26:56.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It copied its data: below the threshold,
Dialogue: 0,0:26:57.06,0:27:01.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}each run stays in one whirlpool,\Nand the runs split unevenly.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:02.87,0:27:04.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two ways to teach the mirror.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:05.19,0:27:07.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first student, data augmentation,
Dialogue: 0,0:27:07.60,0:27:11.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}sees each training snapshot\Nflipped half the time.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:11.54,0:27:15.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The second, equivariance,\Nhas the mirror built into its wind:
Dialogue: 0,0:27:15.53,0:27:19.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a mirrored noise point always\Nlands on the mirrored snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:20.58,0:27:24.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At a {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} neither has seen,\Nwho stays balanced?
Dialogue: 0,0:27:27.89,0:27:29.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The built-in mirror.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:29.71,0:27:31.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first student tilts a little.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:31.83,0:27:36.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet in the mirror's crowd,\Neach snapshot still points one way.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:36.67,0:27:40.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The mirror balances the\Ncrowd, not each snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:40.52,0:27:45.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Its card goes on the crowd\Nshelf: built in, exactly.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:46.10,0:27:48.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is that tilt, setting by setting.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:49.14,0:27:52.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below the threshold,\Nruns often split unevenly:
Dialogue: 0,0:27:52.67,0:27:57.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}at zero point eight six, only\Nfive of sixteen runs point up.
Dialogue: 0,0:27:58.04,0:28:03.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The plain {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} copies the imbalance,\Nand spills it onto unseen settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:03.93,0:28:07.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Augmentation sits on the {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nat its training settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:07.71,0:28:11.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Between them, it drifts:\Nthe network fills the gaps freely.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:12.34,0:28:15.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The built-in mirror stays\Non the floor everywhere.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:15.58,0:28:19.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Flipped copies teach the mirror\Nonly where there are data.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:20.10,0:28:22.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Can the energy budget catch the tilt?
Dialogue: 0,0:28:22.73,0:28:26.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. Each whirlpool\Nbalances its own budget.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:26.52,0:28:29.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So a mix of the two passes,\Nin any proportion.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:30.26,0:28:35.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On a check of crowd laws, the most\Nlopsided machine even edges ahead.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:35.53,0:28:37.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The mirror needs its own tool.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:38.80,0:28:40.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Remember the budget of the shoebox.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:41.08,0:28:45.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Snapshot by snapshot,\Nenergy rises or falls.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:45.48,0:28:50.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Only the crowd balances, on average,\Nlike a bank account over the years.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:51.51,0:28:54.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now, a tempting fix for the machine.
Dialogue: 0,0:28:54.64,0:29:00.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Force every generated snapshot to balance\Nits budget exactly. Better, or worse?
Dialogue: 0,0:29:05.11,0:29:06.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Worse.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:06.26,0:29:10.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Push each snapshot until\Nits energy stops changing:
Dialogue: 0,0:29:10.14,0:29:13.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the wide pile collapses\Ninto a spike at zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:14.12,0:29:17.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A ledger where every single day balances.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:17.68,0:29:19.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No real account looks like that.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:20.62,0:29:24.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nto the truth grows ninefold.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:24.78,0:29:28.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A short proof says why:\Neven a perfect generator,
Dialogue: 0,0:29:28.22,0:29:31.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}forced this way, cannot match the truth.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:31.73,0:29:34.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the budget goes on the crowd shelf.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:34.55,0:29:39.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the next test bench, a cruder\Nscore is fooled by such a spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:40.20,0:29:43.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Impose the budget on the\Ncrowd, during training.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:43.68,0:29:47.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Draw a batch of snapshots at\Nany {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&}, even without data.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:48.08,0:29:51.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Average their energy change\Nand square it: this penalty,
Dialogue: 0,0:29:52.03,0:29:56.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the crowd loss, vanishes\Nonly when the crowd balances.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:56.91,0:29:58.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But there is a trap.
Dialogue: 0,0:29:58.55,0:30:02.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Even for a balanced crowd,\Na batch's average wobbles;
Dialogue: 0,0:30:02.23,0:30:04.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}its square grows with the scatter.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:05.12,0:30:07.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So training also squeezes the scatter,
Dialogue: 0,0:30:07.73,0:30:11.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}nudging every snapshot toward\Nbalance: toward the spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:12.53,0:30:15.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It showed up while\Nbuilding this experiment.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:15.74,0:30:18.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The fix: split the batch\Ninto two independent
Dialogue: 0,0:30:18.92,0:30:21.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}juries and multiply their averages.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:22.13,0:30:27.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On average, the scatter drops out,\Nleaving only the crowd's imbalance.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:27.61,0:30:31.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Does it pay off? The next tests will tell.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:32.47,0:30:35.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In three dimensions,\Nwhich budgets should generated
Dialogue: 0,0:30:35.56,0:30:39.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}fields meet on average,\Nand must their spread match too?
Dialogue: 0,0:30:44.54,0:30:48.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}New test bench: whole\Nfields, not three dials.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:48.95,0:30:53.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A flat square of conducting fluid,\Nits edges wrapping around.
Dialogue: 0,0:30:53.52,0:30:56.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In blue, the swirl of\Nthe {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&}; in vermillion,
Dialogue: 0,0:30:56.60,0:30:59.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the electric currents behind the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:00.28,0:31:04.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One knob, {\c&H7AAF1B&}eta{\c&HF7F4F2&}: how fast the field smudges.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:04.24,0:31:07.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But a flat world cannot sustain a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:07.43,0:31:10.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here a random forcing\Nkeeps the field alive:
Dialogue: 0,0:31:10.62,0:31:13.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a training ground for laws, not a dynamo.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:14.97,0:31:19.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Every training field passes the\Nbox test: it is divergence-free.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:19.76,0:31:21.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Train a plain {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&} on them.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:22.63,0:31:25.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Will its fields be divergence-free?
Dialogue: 0,0:31:28.75,0:31:29.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:29.75,0:31:32.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now lines appear and\Nvanish inside the box.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:33.15,0:31:35.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Relative to the field's own variations,
Dialogue: 0,0:31:36.01,0:31:40.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the divergence is zero point\Nfour one: a defect of order one.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:41.55,0:31:44.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A soft penalty only lowers\Nit to zero point three
Dialogue: 0,0:31:44.86,0:31:48.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}four for the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&}, zero\Npoint one six for the field.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:48.97,0:31:53.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the magnetic energy comes out\Ntwo point seven times too high.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:53.46,0:31:56.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This law belongs on the\Nshelf for every snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:31:57.84,0:32:00.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First fix: draw a hill map, not arrows,
Dialogue: 0,0:32:00.80,0:32:04.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and let the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} run\Nalong its contour lines.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:05.02,0:32:07.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the lines crowd, it runs faster,
Dialogue: 0,0:32:07.66,0:32:11.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so nothing piles up or leaks,\Nwhatever the landscape.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:12.26,0:32:16.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The box agrees: lines in, lines out.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:16.94,0:32:21.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} draws two hill maps,\Nfor the flow and the field:
Dialogue: 0,0:32:21.09,0:32:25.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}zero divergence by construction,\Nhowever badly it is trained.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:26.18,0:32:32.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Second fix, for any field: split it into\Nswirl plus slope, and delete the slope.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:33.24,0:32:38.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Wave by wave, remove the part of each\Narrow along the wave's own direction.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:39.39,0:32:44.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The gauges fall to rounding error,\Ndown to ten to the minus seventeen.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:44.49,0:32:48.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Measured, though, with the very\Noperator used to enforce it.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:49.47,0:32:54.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the plain {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}:\Nwhy does it miss, trained on clean data?
Dialogue: 0,0:32:55.14,0:32:57.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Follow the part of a\Nfield breaking the law.
Dialogue: 0,0:32:58.06,0:33:01.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To bring it exactly to zero,\Nthe {\c&HF09CB4&}wind{\c&HF7F4F2&} must act as a spring
Dialogue: 0,0:33:01.93,0:33:06.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}that turns infinitely stiff as\Nthe transport clock runs out.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:07.01,0:33:12.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A bounded network cannot, and a sampler\Nwith finite steps cannot follow either.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:12.82,0:33:17.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A gap of order one remains:\Nalmost is not exactly.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:18.80,0:33:24.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the crowd law: on average,\Nenergy in equals energy out.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:24.58,0:33:28.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three ways to impose it,\Nat settings of {\c&H7AAF1B&}eta{\c&HF7F4F2&} never seen.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:29.51,0:33:34.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One: during training, the crowd\Nloss, with its two juries.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:34.33,0:33:36.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Against a control run of equal length,
Dialogue: 0,0:33:36.62,0:33:41.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&} drops from\Ntwo point five to zero point seven.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:41.53,0:33:43.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The control itself gains nothing.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:44.63,0:33:48.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two: after training,\Nreweight the generated crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:49.28,0:33:52.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the model is worst,\Na poll of five hundred
Dialogue: 0,0:33:52.48,0:33:56.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}shrinks to one person,\Nand the crowd gets worse.
Dialogue: 0,0:33:56.75,0:34:00.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three: rescale every\Nsnapshot onto the budget.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:01.14,0:34:04.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each snapshot now dissipates\Nthe same: a spike,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:04.36,0:34:07.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}under a thousandth of the true spread.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:07.61,0:34:13.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Yet the same {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nbuilt on spectra, improves: it is fooled.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:13.68,0:34:18.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Only the spread gives the spike away.\NThe crowd loss shows no spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:19.52,0:34:22.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One training seed each,\Ninterpolation only,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:22.63,0:34:25.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}every model far above the noise floor.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:26.74,0:34:29.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the two shelves, now full.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:29.54,0:34:33.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For every snapshot: zero\Ndivergence. Build it in,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:33.38,0:34:38.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}or project, and it holds exactly;\Na penalty only roughly.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:38.93,0:34:42.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For the crowd: the energy budget.\NImpose it on the crowd
Dialogue: 0,0:34:42.70,0:34:47.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}during training. Reweighting afterwards\Nfails where the model is poor;
Dialogue: 0,0:34:47.41,0:34:50.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}forcing it onto each\Nsnapshot makes a spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:34:51.44,0:34:56.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the mirror? Each snapshot is\Nlopsided, so it is a crowd law,
Dialogue: 0,0:34:56.22,0:34:59.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}yet a network can carry\Nit exactly, built in.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:00.66,0:35:05.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One rule sorts them all:\Nenforce each law where it is true.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:08.34,0:35:12.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Put the pieces together:\Nthe machine this study proposes.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:12.32,0:35:17.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A {\c&HF09CB4&}wind{\c&HF7F4F2&} carries noise to a symmetric\Ncrowd of codes, mirror built in.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:17.70,0:35:23.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A decoder turns each code into hill maps,\Nso every field is divergence-free.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:23.62,0:35:26.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And the energy budget\Nis imposed on the crowd.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:27.15,0:35:31.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It has not been built as a whole:\Nthe experiments tested pieces,
Dialogue: 0,0:35:31.33,0:35:36.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on three dials and a flat sheet, with\Nsmall networks on an ordinary processor.
Dialogue: 0,0:35:37.50,0:35:43.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One question remains: from snapshots,\Nhow well can we answer {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&}?
Dialogue: 0,0:35:44.17,0:35:48.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In the partners' solver snapshots,\Nwhich laws hold exactly,
Dialogue: 0,0:35:48.12,0:35:50.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and under which discrete operator?
Dialogue: 0,0:35:55.77,0:35:59.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The toll counter is back:\Nevery car is now {\c&HF09CB4&}generated{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:00.28,0:36:03.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}They come from the machine\Nwith the built-in mirror:
Dialogue: 0,0:36:03.42,0:36:05.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}twenty thousand at each of forty-one
Dialogue: 0,0:36:05.59,0:36:08.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}settings of {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nmost unseen in training.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:08.99,0:36:13.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Rice, with the equations,\Nturns them into a {\c&H00A1ED&}crossing rate{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:13.82,0:36:16.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Against the counted truth,\Nfar from the threshold,
Dialogue: 0,0:36:17.02,0:36:19.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&H00A1ED&}cumulative error{\c&HF7F4F2&} is fourteen percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:20.41,0:36:23.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Setting by setting, the miss\Naverages seventeen percent,
Dialogue: 0,0:36:24.03,0:36:27.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and reaches sixty-five\Npercent at two settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:27.97,0:36:31.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near the threshold, the error\Nclimbs to forty-two percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:32.69,0:36:35.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One model, one training seed.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:35.40,0:36:39.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Rice needs the equations,\Nand gives a rate, not waiting times.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:40.05,0:36:44.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So, the study's third question:\Ncan a machine tell us {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&}?
Dialogue: 0,0:36:44.93,0:36:50.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For crossings on three dials: far from the\Nthreshold, roughly yes; near it, not yet.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:53.07,0:36:57.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Zoom in. The truth is silent\Nup to zero point eight six.
Dialogue: 0,0:36:58.34,0:37:01.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But the machine changes\Nsmoothly with {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:01.84,0:37:05.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Will its crossing rate switch\Non early, late, or on time?
Dialogue: 0,0:37:09.65,0:37:12.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Early and late, but never on time.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:13.06,0:37:15.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Below the threshold,\Nit leaks false crossings:
Dialogue: 0,0:37:16.06,0:37:18.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}up to a tenth of the rate just above.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:19.24,0:37:24.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At zero point eight six five, it is late:\Nless than a sixth of the counted rate.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:25.50,0:37:28.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Just above, it overshoots:\Nfive crossings per
Dialogue: 0,0:37:28.79,0:37:32.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}thousand time units,\Nagainst three point four.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:33.54,0:37:36.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Why? Picture the machine as a stiff sheet:
Dialogue: 0,0:37:37.04,0:37:41.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}as {\c&H7AAF1B&}Gamma{\c&HF7F4F2&} turns, its crowd\Nchanges only so fast.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:41.62,0:37:47.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The truth climbs a steep ramp: just above\Nzero point eight six, the corridor opens.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:49.09,0:37:53.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The sheet rounds the corner:\Ntoo early, then too low.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:53.46,0:37:59.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The data show both: early strays in the\Ncorridor, then too few at the threshold.
Dialogue: 0,0:37:59.98,0:38:05.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A theorem: at a true jump, a smooth\Nmachine misses by at least half of it.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:05.96,0:38:10.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Unless it is very supple,\Nor told which state the system is in.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:11.10,0:38:17.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here, a ramp, not a jump, and one seed:\Nconsistent with the theorem, not proof.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:18.40,0:38:21.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Nor does the theorem\Nexplain the overshoot.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:22.47,0:38:26.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The crowd loss, on three\Ndials, at unseen settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:26.81,0:38:30.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the good machine,\Nit halves the crowd-law residuals,
Dialogue: 0,0:38:30.35,0:38:32.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}even for laws it never enforced.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:33.23,0:38:39.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But the spread of energy changes shrinks\Nby a quarter: a faint echo of the spike.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:39.78,0:38:43.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On a deliberately biased\Nmachine, it fails:
Dialogue: 0,0:38:43.19,0:38:46.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}one run diverged, the other got worse.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:46.95,0:38:49.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the crossing rate, with one seed:
Dialogue: 0,0:38:49.44,0:38:53.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}slightly better near the\Nthreshold, worse far from it.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:54.94,0:38:57.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the strictest judge: the {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:38:58.16,0:39:01.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On three dials, the best\Nmachine sits at about
Dialogue: 0,0:39:01.22,0:39:04.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}twice its floor;\Nbut three numbers are easy.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:04.78,0:39:08.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the flat sheet of plasma,\Nevery model's spectra miss
Dialogue: 0,0:39:08.87,0:39:13.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}far more than two data samples\Ndiffer: at best, sixfold.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:14.11,0:39:20.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two seeds on the dials, one on the sheet;\Nthe PhD plan asks for at least three.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:20.67,0:39:23.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Every test sits between trained settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:24.94,0:39:28.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The scoreboard: eight mechanisms,\Neach with a verdict.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:29.30,0:39:32.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For every snapshot:\Nhill maps and projection,
Dialogue: 0,0:39:32.98,0:39:35.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}exact; the penalty, approximate.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:36.47,0:39:41.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For the crowd: the mirror,\Nexact; the crowd loss,
Dialogue: 0,0:39:41.17,0:39:45.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}useful but not clean; the rest, failures.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:46.18,0:39:48.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the study's three questions.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:48.67,0:39:52.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One: between the dots,\Nthe shapes come out right,
Dialogue: 0,0:39:52.17,0:39:55.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}but no model reaches its {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:39:55.66,0:40:01.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two: each law where it holds,\Nexactly yes, {\c&H00A1ED&}on average{\c&HF7F4F2&} partly.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:02.02,0:40:07.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three, {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&}: roughly right far\Nfrom the threshold, poor near it.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:08.72,0:40:09.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three lessons.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:10.34,0:40:12.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Exact laws: build them in.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:13.34,0:40:18.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Crowd laws: impose them on the\Ncrowd, never on each snapshot.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:18.67,0:40:23.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And judge every result against the\Nnoise floor, with enough seeds.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:25.04,0:40:28.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which settings should get runs\Nlong enough to count reversals,
Dialogue: 0,0:40:28.34,0:40:30.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so a predicted rate can be checked?
Dialogue: 0,0:40:35.57,0:40:38.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So far, every test ran\Non toy models. Before
Dialogue: 0,0:40:38.96,0:40:42.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}real dynamos, back to\Nthe map of the field.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:42.81,0:40:47.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Zoom into its thin data-driven\Nlane: it opens into four tracks.
Dialogue: 0,0:40:48.60,0:40:53.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A laboratory dynamo sets every\Ntrack four tests: three dimensions,
Dialogue: 0,0:40:53.83,0:40:59.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}walls with iron and a vacuum, a tiny\N{\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Prandtl number{\c&HF7F4F2&}, and rare flips.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:01.70,0:41:07.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First track: fast surrogates,\Nnetworks trained to imitate a solver.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:07.45,0:41:12.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Some carry the equations in their loss;\Nothers learn maps between whole fields.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:13.22,0:41:18.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For conducting fluids, they now reach\Nflat, two-dimensional turbulence.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:18.37,0:41:21.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For the Earth, recall the\Ntwenty twenty-two study:
Dialogue: 0,0:41:21.67,0:41:25.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the methods it tried could\Nnot reliably foresee a flip.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:26.36,0:41:30.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Their weak spot: trained to\Nminimize a mean-square error,
Dialogue: 0,0:41:30.22,0:41:33.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}ensembles of them\Nregress to the mean flow.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:33.69,0:41:37.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A cloud with two polarities\Ncollapses to its middle.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:38.37,0:41:40.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Second track: {\c&HF09CB4&}generative models{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:41:40.53,0:41:44.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}which learn the whole cloud,\Nnot just its middle.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:44.63,0:41:47.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Adversarial networks\Nfirst, then diffusion,
Dialogue: 0,0:41:47.68,0:41:51.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}then flow matching,\Nthe family the study used.
Dialogue: 0,0:41:51.73,0:41:55.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}They now dream three-dimensional\Nturbulence, global weather ensembles,
Dialogue: 0,0:41:56.07,0:41:59.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and steady turbulent\Nstates for chosen {\c&H7AAF1B&}settings{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:00.12,0:42:03.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Trained on dynamo\Nsimulations? We found none.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:05.54,0:42:10.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Third track: making samples\Nobey the laws. Four families.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:11.03,0:42:15.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Hard: build the law into the network,\Nsay a potential whose curl
Dialogue: 0,0:42:15.37,0:42:19.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}has no divergence, or project\Neach sample while drawing it.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:20.09,0:42:25.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Soft: a penalty in the loss,\Nobeyed only approximately.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:25.24,0:42:28.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution{\c&HF7F4F2&}: constrain the crowd,
Dialogue: 0,0:42:28.29,0:42:33.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}by its covariance, or by the\Nequation a steady cloud obeys.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:33.46,0:42:38.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And symmetry: an equivariant\Nflow gives a symmetric cloud.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:39.44,0:42:43.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A twenty twenty-six preprint\Ncompared ways to impose zero
Dialogue: 0,0:42:43.40,0:42:47.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}divergence on flat flows:\Nno single strategy dominates.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:48.00,0:42:53.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In three dimensions, we found two\Nexactly divergence-free generators,
Dialogue: 0,0:42:53.13,0:42:56.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}both preprints: periodic-box turbulence,
Dialogue: 0,0:42:56.11,0:42:59.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and a current-free field\Nin the Sun's corona.
Dialogue: 0,0:42:59.88,0:43:04.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}None handles walls, a vacuum\Noutside and iron inside.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:05.64,0:43:10.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Fourth track: data assimilation,\Nmerging a model with a few measurements.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:11.22,0:43:16.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Learned priors came in through compressed\Nlatent spaces, then diffusion models.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:16.76,0:43:22.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And a library now plugs deep networks into\NKalman filters and variational methods.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:22.89,0:43:26.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}We found no diffusion prior used\Nthis way for conducting fluids.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:27.22,0:43:29.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near a flip, the answer\Nhas two polarities,
Dialogue: 0,0:43:29.89,0:43:34.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and a prior that ignores the\Nmirror could bias it toward one.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:35.07,0:43:39.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the rings: papers\Nco-signed by the supervisors.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:39.81,0:43:45.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Dynamo side: Taylor–Green dynamos pushed\Nto small {\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Prandtl numbers{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:43:45.33,0:43:50.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the VKS tank with its iron\Nimpellers, simulated in two codes.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:50.55,0:43:54.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In twenty twenty-six, both\Ncodes produced VKS reversals,
Dialogue: 0,0:43:54.72,0:43:58.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and a four-dial model\Nreproduces their main features.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:59.76,0:44:03.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Learning side: autoencoder\Nlatent spaces read through
Dialogue: 0,0:44:03.67,0:44:07.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}classical flow modes,\Nvon Kármán flow included.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:07.75,0:44:10.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And assimilation and\Ndiffusion models built
Dialogue: 0,0:44:10.62,0:44:13.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on deep networks,\Noutside conducting fluids.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:15.02,0:44:18.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three ingredients are in hand.\NBut in what we found,
Dialogue: 0,0:44:18.14,0:44:22.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}none of the four has published a\N{\c&HF09CB4&}generative model{\c&HF7F4F2&} trained on dynamo data.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:23.90,0:44:26.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the {\c&HF09CB4&}generative{\c&HF7F4F2&} and\Nconstraint tracks cross
Dialogue: 0,0:44:26.67,0:44:29.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the dynamo tracks, we found the map empty.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:29.90,0:44:33.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}An independent thesis\Nproposal aims at that spot:
Dialogue: 0,0:44:33.36,0:44:36.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}five gaps, each beside\Nits nearest neighbour.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:37.60,0:44:40.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One: a generator of dynamo fields.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:41.28,0:44:44.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Two: {\c&HE58739&}velocity{\c&HF7F4F2&} and\Nmagnetic field with exactly
Dialogue: 0,0:44:44.57,0:44:49.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}zero divergence, in a walled\Ncylinder with iron and vacuum.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:50.03,0:44:53.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Three: energy budgets\Nimposed on the {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution{\c&HF7F4F2&},
Dialogue: 0,0:44:53.57,0:44:57.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}with no target value,\Neven where there are no data.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:57.62,0:45:02.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Four: the flip rate read from\Nsnapshots with Rice's formula.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:02.68,0:45:07.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Five: one generator that\Ncrosses the dynamo threshold.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:08.19,0:45:13.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The study tested pieces on toy cases:\Nexact potentials on a flat sheet,
Dialogue: 0,0:45:13.56,0:45:18.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a budget imposed without data,\NRice's formula on three dials.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:19.54,0:45:23.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The field moves fast: a twenty twenty-six\Nconference paper claims to be the
Dialogue: 0,0:45:23.90,0:45:28.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}first to recognize a trade-off between\Nphysical and {\c&H00A1ED&}distributional{\c&HF7F4F2&} accuracy.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:29.08,0:45:32.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The study's lesson,\Nnever force a crowd law
Dialogue: 0,0:45:32.22,0:45:35.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on each snapshot,\Nmust be placed against it.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:36.51,0:45:39.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Not claimed: waiting times between flips,
Dialogue: 0,0:45:39.73,0:45:45.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}sodium's tiny {\c&H7AAF1B&}magnetic Prandtl number{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nthe Earth's core, real laboratory data.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:46.66,0:45:49.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each empty spot is a\Nsearch result, not a proof,
Dialogue: 0,0:45:49.93,0:45:52.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}to be checked again before every paper.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:54.47,0:45:57.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First VKS data: should\Nthe generator train on the
Dialogue: 0,0:45:58.05,0:46:01.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}LATU periodic grid,\Nor the SFEMaNS cylinder mesh?
Dialogue: 0,0:46:07.00,0:46:08.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the staircase.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:08.85,0:46:12.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first two steps are\Nlit by small experiments:
Dialogue: 0,0:46:12.28,0:46:14.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}three numbers, then a flat sheet.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:15.38,0:46:19.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The third, the twisting\Nbox, has only a preview.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:20.19,0:46:22.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}From there up, every step is a bet,
Dialogue: 0,0:46:22.89,0:46:28.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}written down before the data exist,\Nin an independent PhD proposal.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:29.44,0:46:32.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}To climb, the study first\Nbuilt a factory for the data.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:33.19,0:46:35.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A recipe card sets the {\c&H7AAF1B&}knobs{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:35.66,0:46:40.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Ovens run one simulation per setting,\Non a laptop or a cluster.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:40.85,0:46:43.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each result is sealed in a labelled jar:
Dialogue: 0,0:46:43.77,0:46:47.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a unique number,\Nthe code version, a checksum.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:48.21,0:46:52.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A ledger records every jar, a menu\Nsplits them for training and testing,
Dialogue: 0,0:46:53.06,0:46:56.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&HF09CB4&}chef{\c&HF7F4F2&} learns, and a\Ncritic scores the result.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:57.36,0:47:00.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}It all runs end to end\Non an ordinary processor,
Dialogue: 0,0:47:00.71,0:47:04.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}never yet on a graphics\Ncard or a real cluster.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:04.91,0:47:09.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A grey tag names the file\Neach empty frame awaits.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:10.57,0:47:13.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First product off the\Nline: the flat sheet,
Dialogue: 0,0:47:13.84,0:47:17.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}eleven {\c&H7AAF1B&}diffusivities{\c&HF7F4F2&}, sixteen runs each.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:17.48,0:47:20.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet, a check on the\Ndata: at every setting,
Dialogue: 0,0:47:20.75,0:47:26.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&H00A1ED&}average dissipation{\c&HF7F4F2&} matches the\Ninjected power, within five percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:27.34,0:47:29.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Bet one: the twisting box.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:30.23,0:47:34.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At thirty-two cubed, the small\Nsweep matched the published windows,
Dialogue: 0,0:47:34.52,0:47:39.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}but near seventeen and a half the\Nsign of growth stayed unresolved.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:39.89,0:47:45.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The campaign doubles the resolution and\Nsteps {\c&H7AAF1B&}Rm{\c&HF7F4F2&} by whole numbers up to forty.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:45.64,0:47:48.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet: growth still\Npositive at seventeen,
Dialogue: 0,0:47:48.78,0:47:52.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}already negative at eighteen,\Nwith two fits agreeing:
Dialogue: 0,0:47:52.56,0:47:57.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}one over the kept part of the run,\None over its last quarter.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:58.78,0:48:02.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: medium. At eighteen,\Nthe small sweep's two
Dialogue: 0,0:48:02.58,0:48:06.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}fits already differed by\Nmore than the rate itself.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:06.98,0:48:12.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Worth asking before the run: add three\Npoints between seventeen and eighteen?
Dialogue: 0,0:48:13.40,0:48:15.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Bet two, the first true dynamo:
Dialogue: 0,0:48:15.88,0:48:19.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a Taylor-Green flow of\Ncounter-rotating eddies.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:20.52,0:48:23.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Now the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} pushes back on the flow.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:23.32,0:48:28.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Above a threshold, a tiny {\c&H3468EB&}seed{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nshould grow, then level off.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:29.00,0:48:33.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Twelve runs cross four Reynolds\Nnumbers with three values of {\c&H7AAF1B&}Pm{\c&HF7F4F2&}.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:34.01,0:48:39.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bet: a threshold in {\c&H7AAF1B&}Rm{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nbracketed for at least two values of Pm.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:41.45,0:48:46.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: medium. Published runs\Ndefine the Reynolds number differently,
Dialogue: 0,0:48:46.57,0:48:49.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so the threshold may fall off this grid.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:50.14,0:48:54.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near threshold, a run may still be\Ngrowing when its snapshots are kept.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:55.30,0:48:58.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Lengthen those runs,\Nor flag and exclude them?
Dialogue: 0,0:49:00.10,0:49:02.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Some things need no bet.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:02.48,0:49:05.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In three dimensions,\Nhill maps become potentials:
Dialogue: 0,0:49:05.63,0:49:09.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&} draws them,\Nthen takes their curl.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:09.20,0:49:12.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}So the {\c&HE58739&}flow{\c&HF7F4F2&} and the field stay\Ndivergence-free to rounding,
Dialogue: 0,0:49:12.90,0:49:14.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}even with untrained weights.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:15.72,0:49:20.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Mirror the starting noise, and the\Nsample comes out with its {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} flipped.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:20.79,0:49:23.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That shows the mechanism, not the quality.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:24.87,0:49:26.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Everything else must be measured.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:27.23,0:49:30.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First test: does the {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nget the energy spectra right,
Dialogue: 0,0:49:30.88,0:49:34.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}within a tenth of a decade,\Nat the settings it trained on?
Dialogue: 0,0:49:35.48,0:49:37.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Confidence: low.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:37.67,0:49:41.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In two dimensions, the best\Nmodels only just got there,
Dialogue: 0,0:49:41.62,0:49:47.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and here each run keeps forty snapshots,\Nagainst five thousand in the plan.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:49.27,0:49:53.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Leave the periodic box for a real\Nvessel, and exact gets harder.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:54.13,0:49:57.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The potential needs a\Ngauge and a wall condition;
Dialogue: 0,0:49:57.29,0:49:59.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}then each {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} has exactly one.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:00.43,0:50:05.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}At the soft-iron impellers, {\c&H3468EB&}field lines{\c&HF7F4F2&}\Nbend sharply at the iron's surface,
Dialogue: 0,0:50:05.39,0:50:09.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}so a smooth potential cannot be\Nexact: it needs a kink there.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:10.99,0:50:15.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Outside, the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} must match a\Ncurrent-free field in the vacuum.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:15.75,0:50:17.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The rule is linear but nonlocal:
Dialogue: 0,0:50:17.79,0:50:22.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the {\c&H3468EB&}field{\c&HF7F4F2&} at one point of the\Nwall depends on the whole wall.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:22.44,0:50:27.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Imposing it exactly and cheaply,\Nfor every sample, is still open.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:28.44,0:50:31.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The top steps are\Nsimulations of real machines:
Dialogue: 0,0:50:31.97,0:50:36.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}the VKS cylinder, and DRESDYN,\Na precessing cylinder.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:37.02,0:50:38.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Their bets are already written.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:39.13,0:50:43.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For VKS: the four main patterns\Nwith the right energy shares,
Dialogue: 0,0:50:43.23,0:50:47.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and Rice's reversal rate inside\Nthe simulated count's error bars.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:48.29,0:50:53.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For DRESDYN: the threshold at an unseen\Nprecession rate, within ten percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:55.17,0:50:58.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Those simulations belong\Nto the project's partners,
Dialogue: 0,0:50:58.20,0:51:00.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}are not public, and must be requested.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:02.34,0:51:06.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Before the Taylor-Green runs, which\Nforcing, Reynolds number definition
Dialogue: 0,0:51:06.36,0:51:10.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and resolution make their threshold\Ncomparable with published ones?
Dialogue: 0,0:51:15.50,0:51:18.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Every chapter so far\Nhas ended on a question.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:18.75,0:51:20.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The empty seats raised more.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:21.62,0:51:24.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Hang each one under the step it shapes.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:24.65,0:51:29.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Bet one: whether to add settings\Nbetween seventeen and eighteen.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:29.58,0:51:34.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Bet two: the {\c&H3468EB&}seed{\c&HF7F4F2&}, the Reynolds number,\Nthe length of each run.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:34.91,0:51:37.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The {\c&HF09CB4&}generator{\c&HF7F4F2&}: what it must match.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:37.52,0:51:40.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The top steps: the partners' data.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:40.88,0:51:44.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A staircase of bets is a\Nstaircase of decisions,
Dialogue: 0,0:51:44.15,0:51:47.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the first ones come before any run.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:48.97,0:51:53.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}First, the runs: these answers\Nchange what gets computed.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:54.15,0:51:59.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Near a threshold, start from a\Ntiny {\c&H3468EB&}seed{\c&HF7F4F2&}, a strong one, or both?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:00.18,0:52:03.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which values of {\c&H7AAF1B&}Pm{\c&HF7F4F2&}, since\Nsodium's is out of reach?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:04.12,0:52:07.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Settings spread evenly,\Nor packed near each threshold?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:08.23,0:52:10.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How many independent\Nsnapshots per setting?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:11.20,0:52:14.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}How long must runs near a threshold last?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:14.89,0:52:17.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which forcing, Reynolds\Nnumber and grid make the
Dialogue: 0,0:52:17.95,0:52:21.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Taylor-Green threshold\Ncomparable with published ones?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:22.11,0:52:24.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And which campaign comes first?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:25.27,0:52:31.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Then the framing: how each result will\Nbe judged, decided before the data exist.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:31.57,0:52:35.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which statistics must generated\Nfields match, down to the shape of
Dialogue: 0,0:52:35.66,0:52:40.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}their field lines, and how close to\Nthe {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&} counts as success?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:40.83,0:52:45.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which budgets must hold {\c&H00A1ED&}on average{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nand must their spread match too?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:46.37,0:52:51.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One answer, not about physics,\Ncould make most renting unnecessary.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:51.42,0:52:52.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which question is it?
Dialogue: 0,0:52:55.97,0:52:57.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Where the computing happens.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:58.08,0:53:02.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}If a lab has graphics cards an\Nintern can use, or there is already
Dialogue: 0,0:53:02.42,0:53:06.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}a Jean Zay project to join,\Nrenting shrinks to a short trial.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:07.12,0:53:13.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And for the top steps: which partner\Nsimulations, in what form, and by when?
Dialogue: 0,0:53:14.14,0:53:16.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The second shelf matters\Nfor the internship plan
Dialogue: 0,0:53:16.87,0:53:20.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the data request,\Nnot for the first campaigns.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:20.66,0:53:23.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which settings get runs long\Nenough to count reversals?
Dialogue: 0,0:53:24.31,0:53:27.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which operator decides what exact means?
Dialogue: 0,0:53:27.75,0:53:30.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Which labels must each snapshot carry?
Dialogue: 0,0:53:30.72,0:53:33.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And may new results appear in this film?
Dialogue: 0,0:53:33.97,0:53:39.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The last shelf can wait: the top steps'\Ndetails, and how to frame a first paper.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:41.10,0:53:45.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The first shelf fits in one message,\Nto write before renting anything.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:46.33,0:53:48.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}This ranking is only a proposal.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:48.66,0:53:50.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The supervisors may well change it.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:51.53,0:53:53.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Several answers could\Nchange what gets computed,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:53.97,0:53:56.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}or whether anything is rented at all.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:56.55,0:53:58.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The cheapest experiment\Nis a good question,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:59.04,0:54:01.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}asked before renting a graphics card.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:06.94,0:54:09.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Questions first, then computing.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:09.52,0:54:12.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Here is what those questions\Nrest on, in two columns.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:13.20,0:54:18.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the left, what three small experiments\Nmeasured on an ordinary processor.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:18.33,0:54:20.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Toy problems, one or two training seeds,
Dialogue: 0,0:54:20.75,0:54:25.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and machines tested only between\Nthe settings they were trained on.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:25.88,0:54:30.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On the right, what the staircase bets,\Neach with the rule that will settle it.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:31.64,0:54:35.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Each lamp shows a verdict only\Nonce its data file arrives,
Dialogue: 0,0:54:35.38,0:54:37.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}and the film is updated with it.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:39.40,0:54:42.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Back to the compass,\Nand to the two questions.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:43.55,0:54:45.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}When will this needle turn?
Dialogue: 0,0:54:45.65,0:54:47.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}For the Earth, no one can say.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:48.48,0:54:51.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}In a simple computer\Nmodel, chaos makes the
Dialogue: 0,0:54:51.23,0:54:54.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}forecast fade before\Nthe average wait ends.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:54.71,0:55:00.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}But {\c&H00A1ED&}how often{\c&HF7F4F2&} the field flips, counted as\Ncrossings of zero, can still be answered.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:00.89,0:55:05.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The answer hides in a shoebox of\Nsnapshots, read with the equations.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:06.34,0:55:11.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}And a {\c&HF09CB4&}machine{\c&HF7F4F2&} can learn to dream such\Nsnapshots, between the simulated settings.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:13.07,0:55:15.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On small test problems, it partly works.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:16.24,0:55:19.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The mirror holds by construction,\Nat every setting.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:19.65,0:55:22.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}On three dials, the {\c&H00A1ED&}rate of zero crossings{\c&HF7F4F2&}
Dialogue: 0,0:55:22.32,0:55:26.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}is roughly right far from the\Nthreshold, and poor near it.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:26.62,0:55:29.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A smooth machine rounds\Noff a steep change.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:30.07,0:55:33.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The bigger tests,\Nin three dimensions and in the
Dialogue: 0,0:55:33.08,0:55:36.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}shapes of real machines,\Nare written as bets.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:37.57,0:55:39.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One lesson remains.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:39.33,0:55:41.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Enforce each law where it is true.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:42.18,0:55:44.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Exactly, where it is exact.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:45.02,0:55:48.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}{\c&H00A1ED&}On average{\c&HF7F4F2&}, where it\Nonly holds on average.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:52.20,0:55:55.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The report, the code,\Nand every result in this film are
Dialogue: 0,0:55:55.46,0:55:58.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}on the project's site,\Nwith their limits written out.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:59.42,0:56:02.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The rest of the staircase\Nis still to be computed.
